  <h1>India’s Health Infrastructure Post-COVID — Gaps and Reforms</h1>
  <div><em>The Hindu | Date: 2024 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>The COVID-19 pandemic exposed India’s fragile health infrastructure, prompting reforms and increased investments. By 2024, The Hindu noted progress in digital health, vaccine manufacturing, and insurance coverage but warned of persistent gaps in funding, human resources, and primary care. For UPSC, health infrastructure links to governance, economy, and social justice.
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Current Status of Health Infrastructure<br/>•	Public Health Expenditure: ~2.1% of GDP (2024), below the global average of 6%.<br/>•	Hospital Beds: ~1.4 per 1,000 population (WHO recommends 3).<br/>•	Doctors: 1 per 1,511 people (WHO norm: 1 per 1,000).<br/>•	Insurance Coverage: Ayushman Bharat PM-JAY covers ~50 crore people.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Achievements Since COVID<br/>1.	Digital Health Mission (2021):<br/>o	Unique Health IDs, e-health records.<br/>o	Expansion of telemedicine.<br/>2.	Vaccine Manufacturing: India became global supplier of COVID vaccines (“Vaccine Maitri”).<br/>3.	Health Infrastructure Upgrades:<br/>o	New oxygen plants, ICU beds, and medical colleges.<br/>4.	Insurance Coverage: Ayushman Bharat expanded financial protection.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Challenges<br/>1.	Low Public Spending: Still far below 2.5% GDP target of National Health Policy (2017).<br/>2.	Human Resource Shortage: Rural areas lack doctors, nurses, and paramedics.<br/>3.	Primary Care Weakness: Health & Wellness Centres under Ayushman Bharat face staff shortages.<br/>4.	Urban–Rural Divide: Tier-2/3 cities and villages lack advanced facilities.<br/>5.	Out-of-Pocket Expenditure: Still ~48% of total health spending (vs global avg ~18%).<br/>________________________________________<br/>Governance & Policy Issues<br/>•	Centre–State Coordination: Health is a state subject, creating variations.<br/>•	Regulatory Gaps: Private healthcare often unregulated, expensive.<br/>•	Insurance Focus: Risk of neglecting preventive and primary care.<br/>________________________________________<br/>UPSC Relevance<br/>•	GS-II (Governance): Health policy, federalism in healthcare.<br/>•	GS-III (Economy & Social Justice): Human development, inclusive growth.<br/>•	Essay:<br/>o	“Health is not a privilege but a fundamental right.”<br/>o	“Pandemics reveal the strength or weakness of governance.”<br/>•	PSIR Paper II: Welfare state and social contract theory.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Raise Spending: Move toward 2.5% of GDP public health expenditure.<br/>2.	Strengthen Primary Care: Upgrade Health & Wellness Centres with staff and digital tools.<br/>3.	Human Resource Development: Expand medical/nursing colleges, incentivize rural service.<br/>4.	Public–Private Partnerships: Regulated PPPs for diagnostics and specialty care.<br/>5.	Preventive Focus: Promote nutrition, sanitation, vaccination, and lifestyle interventions.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>India’s post-COVID health reforms are a step forward but not transformative. Achieving universal health coverage requires systemic strengthening of primary care, human resources, and financing. For UPSC aspirants, health infrastructure exemplifies how social justice, governance, and economy converge in welfare policy.</p>
